  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Fixes\n\nMethods of two interfaces that differ only in object and dynamic (#303, closes #301). Two interfaces' Use(object) and Use(dynamic), or Use(List\\<object>) and Use(List\\<dynamic>), have the same signature at runtime. The imposter didn't treat them as colliding and failed with CS0111. They're now implemented apart and set up through their interfaces' setup views.\n\nMembers with dynamic types (#306, closes #304). These failed to compile because a dynamic argument binds the call at runtime:\n\na method returning dynamic (CS8716);\n\na class's virtual method, property or indexer with a dynamic parameter, value or key, with its base implementation (CS1971, CS1976, CS1977, CS1972);\n\na class constructor taking dynamic (CS1975);\n\na generic method with a dynamic parameter (CS1962).\n\nGenerated code now passes dynamic values between its own members as object. Setups (Arg\\<dynamic>, Returns, callbacks) keep dynamic.\n\nDiagnostics\n\nIMP013: member with a pointer type (#305, closes #302). A member whose signature uses a pointer or function pointer type, such as int\\*, int\\*\\[] or delegate\\*\\<int, void>, generated an imposter with dozens of compile errors and no explanation. Such a target now reports IMP013 and gets no imposter.\n\nA class constructor that takes a pointer is left out of the imposter's constructors.\n\nA class whose accessible constructors all take one reports IMP013.",
